From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: GPU voltage by speed bin

`arm64-dts-a733-gpu-opp-ladder-on-the-gpu0-clk-mux` gave every die the
vendor's vfdefault column, the highest at every rate. Give each bin the
column the vendor ships for it, and keep vfdefault for every die this tree
cannot place.

The bin. The SID word at fuse offset 0x4c carries the speed bin in bits
[15:8] and a second copy in [23:16]; the CPU tables already read it
(`nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse`, read back on several dies). The
GPU gets bits [15:8] of that word as cell gpu_speed_bin (reg = <0x4c 0x4>,
bits = <8 8>); a cell at 0x4d would be unaligned to the SID's nvmem stride
of 4, which fails the registration of the whole provider. The second copy is
not read. The user manual documents the SID's place in the memory map, not
this field.

The columns. The vendor's GPU driver takes the SoC's DVFS code, maps it
through vf_mapping_table and reads opp-microvolt-vf<mapped value> from its
gpu-opp-table (Allwinner BSP: rgx_sunxi/sunxi_platform.c sunxi_get_opp(),
configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1-cpu-vf.dtsi, configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1.dtsi).
For the bins the CPU tables carry:

  fuse  column   800 MHz  1008 MHz
  0x03  vf0300    820 mV    940 mV
  0x04  vf0400    800 mV    900 mV
  0x05  vf0500    800 mV    880 mV
  0x06  vf0600    800 mV    920 mV
  0x14  vf0401    800 mV    880 mV
  0x15  vf0501    800 mV    860 mV
  0x01  vf0100    840 mV    960 mV   (= vfdefault)
  0x02  vf0200    840 mV    960 mV   (= vfdefault)

400 and 600 MHz are 800 mV in every column. Rows are selected with
opp-supported-hw, one bit per raw fuse value; the vfdefault rows carry every
bit except the six bins with rows of their own (0xffcfff87), so 0x00, 0x11,
0x12 and any value without a row get the highest voltage. Every bin has
exactly one row per rate. All values lie in VDD-GPU's recommended range
(A733 datasheet V1.01, Table 5-2, PDF p. 94).

Bins read on A733 dies so far: 0x04 and 0x05. The vendor stack has never run
these columns on them; it runs the GPU fixed at 600 MHz.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
@@ -1129,6 +1129,17 @@
 			};
 
 			/*
+			 * The same speed bin for the GPU: bits [15:8] of the word
+			 * above. The cell is word-aligned, as the SID's nvmem
+			 * stride of 4 requires; the second copy in [23:16] is
+			 * not read.
+			 */
+			gpu_speed_bin: gpu-speed-bin@4c {
+				reg = <0x4c 0x4>;
+				bits = <8 8>;
+			};
+
+			/*
 			 * THS factory calibration, fuse offsets 0x44..0x4f =
 			 * ARM 0x03006244..0x0300624f: ft_temp and one raw
 			 * code per sensor, packed 12-bit (the unpacking is in
@@ -3542,6 +3553,8 @@
 			assigned-clock-rates = <0>, <1008000000>;
 			operating-points-v2 = <&gpu_opp_table>;
 			power-supply = <&reg_dcdc4>;
+			nvmem-cells = <&gpu_speed_bin>;
+			nvmem-cell-names = "speed-bin";
 			#cooling-cells = <2>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
@@ -3550,13 +3563,9 @@
 
 / {
 	/*
-	 * GPU OPPs: the four rows of the vendor's GPU table (Allwinner BSP
-	 * configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1.dtsi, gpu-opp-table) at their
-	 * opp-microvolt-vfdefault values, the column the vendor applies when it
-	 * cannot place a die in a bin. The vendor's per-bin columns are lower
-	 * (down to 860 mV at 1008 MHz); this tree has no way to hand a bin to
-	 * the GPU's OPP table, so it carries the column that is safe for every
-	 * die.
+	 * GPU OPPs: the four rates of the vendor's GPU table (Allwinner BSP
+	 * configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1.dtsi, gpu-opp-table) with the voltage of
+	 * the die's speed bin.
 	 *
 	 * Every voltage lies in VDD-GPU's recommended range, 0.80-0.96 V (A733
 	 * datasheet V1.01, Table 5-2, PDF p. 94), the same window the board's
@@ -3566,9 +3575,40 @@
 	 * -- the vendor's driver lists the same PERI0 taps as its rate sources.
 	 * Rates from the mask divider are not used (ccu-sun60iw2.c, GPU block):
 	 * a masked rate needs the voltage of its source, not of its average.
-	 * 600 MHz at 800 mV is also the operating point the vendor stack runs
-	 * on this silicon, and 1008 MHz at 960 mV the one this tree pinned
-	 * before it had a ladder.
+	 *
+	 * Bins. drm/imagination reads the bin from gpu_speed_bin and keeps the
+	 * OPPs whose opp-supported-hw has BIT(bin); the bit position is the
+	 * raw fuse value, as for the CPU tables. The voltage per bin is the
+	 * vendor's: its GPU driver takes the SoC's DVFS code (DVFS_EFUSE_OFF
+	 * 0x4c), maps it through vf_mapping_table (sun60iw2p1-cpu-vf.dtsi, the
+	 * table the CPU tables above follow) and uses opp-microvolt-vf<mapped
+	 * value> (Allwinner BSP, rgx_sunxi/sunxi_platform.c, sunxi_get_opp()). The
+	 * bins carried are the CPU tables' set, with their columns:
+	 *
+	 *   fuse  column   800 MHz  1008 MHz
+	 *   0x03  vf0300    820 mV    940 mV
+	 *   0x04  vf0400    800 mV    900 mV
+	 *   0x05  vf0500    800 mV    880 mV
+	 *   0x06  vf0600    800 mV    920 mV
+	 *   0x14  vf0401    800 mV    880 mV
+	 *   0x15  vf0501    800 mV    860 mV
+	 *   0x01  vf0100    840 mV    960 mV   = vfdefault
+	 *   0x02  vf0200    840 mV    960 mV   = vfdefault
+	 *
+	 * 400 and 600 MHz are 800 mV in every column. Every bin not named
+	 * above -- 0x00 (an unprogrammed fuse reads the same), 0x11, 0x12
+	 * (which have no CPU column here), any other value, and a die with the
+	 * second fuse copy programmed -- gets vfdefault, the column the vendor
+	 * uses when it cannot place a die, and the highest at every rate. The
+	 * fallback rows therefore carry every bit but the six bins with rows
+	 * of their own: 0xffcfff87.
+	 *
+	 * Bins read on A733 dies so far: 0x04 and 0x05 (CPU table comment
+	 * above). The vendor stack never applied these columns on them: its
+	 * GPU DVFS is compiled out, the GPU runs 600 MHz at the 800 mV the
+	 * rail boots with. Both bins have run all four rates at their own
+	 * column; before this, every die ran fixed at 1008 MHz and 960 mV,
+	 * the default column.
 	 */
 	gpu_opp_table: opp-table-gpu {
 		compatible = "operating-points-v2";
@@ -3576,21 +3616,69 @@
 		opp-400000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <400000000>;
 			opp-microvolt = <800000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0xffffffff>;
 		};
 
 		opp-600000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <600000000>;
 			opp-microvolt = <800000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0xffffffff>;
 		};
 
 		opp-800000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <800000000>;
 			opp-microvolt = <840000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0xffcfff87>;
+		};
+
+		opp-800000000-speed3 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <800000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <820000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0x00000008>;
+		};
+
+		/* bins 0x04, 0x05, 0x06, 0x14, 0x15 */
+		opp-800000000-speed4 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <800000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <800000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0x00300070>;
 		};
 
 		opp-1008000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1008000000>;
 			opp-microvolt = <960000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0xffcfff87>;
+		};
+
+		opp-1008000000-speed3 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1008000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <940000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0x00000008>;
+		};
+
+		opp-1008000000-speed4 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1008000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <900000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0x00000010>;
+		};
+
+		/* bins 0x05 and 0x14 */
+		opp-1008000000-speed5 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1008000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <880000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0x00100020>;
+		};
+
+		opp-1008000000-speed6 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1008000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <920000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0x00000040>;
+		};
+
+		opp-1008000000-speed21 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1008000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <860000>;
+			opp-supported-hw = <0x00200000>;
 		};
 	};
 
